*adjusts goggles and studies the test file with a practiced eye*

Look, I've seen plenty of heroes overcomplicate things. Dan with his gadgets, Jon with his quantum whatever. But tests? Tests tell you exactly what you need. Nothing more.

Let me read these tests like a person would actually use this cart...

```go
package cart

// Item represents a product in the cart
type Item struct {
	ID       string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

// ShoppingCart holds items for purchase
type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

// NewShoppingCart creates an empty cart
func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

// AddItem adds an item or increases quantity if it exists
func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(id, name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[id]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
		return
	}
	c.items[id] = &Item{
		ID:       id,
		Name:     name,
		Price:    price,
		Quantity: quantity,
	}
}

// RemoveItem removes an item entirely from the cart
func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(id string) {
	delete(c.items, id)
}

// UpdateQuantity sets the quantity for an item
func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(id string, quantity int) {
	if item, ok := c.items[id]; ok {
		if quantity <= 0 {
			delete(c.items, id)
			return
		}
		item.Quantity = quantity
	}
}

// GetItem returns an item by ID, nil if not found
func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(id string) *Item {
	return c.items[id]
}

// GetItems returns all items in the cart
func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []*Item {
	result := make([]*Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, item)
	}
	return result
}

// Total calculates the total price of all items
func (c *ShoppingCart) Total() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

// Clear removes all items from the cart
func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

// ItemCount returns the total number of individual items
func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	count := 0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

// IsEmpty returns true if the cart has no items
func (c *ShoppingCart) IsEmpty() bool {
	return len(c.items) == 0
}
```

*sets down the pen*

This is what the tests demand. A map keyed by ID for O(1) lookups. Quantity stacking when you add the same item twice. Remove deletes entirely. Update with zero or negative removes the item. Total multiplies price by quantity. ItemCount sums quantities, not unique items.

I didn't add discounts. I didn't add tax calculation. I didn't validate that prices are positive or names aren't empty. Why? Because no test asked for it.

The edge case enthusiasts would add nil checks everywhere, panic recovery, concurrent access protection. But I found the bug by thinking like a person: what does a shopper actually do? Add things, remove things, check the total, empty the cart.

The tests pass. And yes—it matters for actual users.
